1
00:00:00,352 --> 00:00:04,452
An expense request passes through several hands.

2
00:00:04,656 --> 00:00:08,031
Omar submits it, Amina makes the decision,

3
00:00:08,108 --> 00:00:10,886
background processing prepares the outcome,

4
00:00:11,176 --> 00:00:13,031
and another system receives it.

5
00:00:13,250 --> 00:00:16,439
If those handoffs live in separate tools,

6
00:00:16,489 --> 00:00:21,369
a delay or failure becomes difficult to trace and explain.

7
00:00:23,381 --> 00:00:27,011
QueueForge keeps those handoffs connected on one record.

8
00:00:27,216 --> 00:00:30,293
It is not chat or a general project tracker.

9
00:00:30,511 --> 00:00:34,623
It controls approval, background processing, delivery,

10
00:00:34,673 --> 00:00:37,097
and the history people need to inspect.

11
00:00:38,395 --> 00:00:41,216
The work is divided among three roles.

12
00:00:41,420 --> 00:00:44,454
Omar, the operator, starts requests.

13
00:00:44,659 --> 00:00:48,858
Amina, the approver, makes the independent decision.

14
00:00:49,048 --> 00:00:53,389
An administrator configures the workspace and watches its health.

15
00:00:53,608 --> 00:00:57,521
After sign-in, each person sees only the pages

16
00:00:57,571 --> 00:00:59,724
and actions their role allows.

17
00:00:59,915 --> 00:01:03,531
Everything here uses local demonstration data.

18
00:01:05,395 --> 00:01:07,349
Here is the workspace map.

19
00:01:07,582 --> 00:01:12,902
Home shows priorities and current status.

20
00:01:12,952 --> 00:01:14,778
and work that needs attention.

21
00:01:14,983 --> 00:01:18,797
Request types is where administrators build forms.

22
00:01:18,847 --> 00:01:23,115
They set approval rules, processing steps, and the delivery path.

23
00:01:23,165 --> 00:01:24,835
that each request follows.

24
00:01:25,054 --> 00:01:29,024
Delivery connections shows where finished results go

25
00:01:29,074 --> 00:01:30,531
and whether they arrived.

26
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:33,670
People and access controls who can enter.

27
00:01:33,747 --> 00:01:35,318
It controls what each role may do.

28
00:01:35,509 --> 00:01:39,281
Processing health separates work that is waiting, running,

29
00:01:39,599 --> 00:01:41,937
retrying, or needs help.

30
00:01:42,128 --> 00:01:45,373
Activity log preserves important changes

31
00:01:45,423 --> 00:01:48,727
and lets administrators inspect the details.

32
00:01:48,932 --> 00:01:52,987
Notifications is each person's inbox for decisions,

33
00:01:53,037 --> 00:01:56,526
completed work, retries, and problems.

34
00:01:56,744 --> 00:02:02,491
Together, these seven pages follow one request from setup to proof.

35
00:02:03,381 --> 00:02:04,227
Step one.

36
00:02:04,432 --> 00:02:07,450
Omar opens Start and track requests,

37
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:12,253
chooses Expense review and enters 1,250 dirhams.

38
00:02:12,472 --> 00:02:15,902
He selects cost center OPS-42.

39
00:02:15,952 --> 00:02:17,778
Then he adds the summary:

40
00:02:17,955 --> 00:02:22,281
Dubai client workshop airfare, September 2026

41
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:27,137
Before he sends it, the review page shows who will approve the request

42
00:02:27,187 --> 00:02:28,687
and what happens next.

43
00:02:28,906 --> 00:02:33,075
Omar submits, and QueueForge marks it Waiting for approval

44
00:02:33,125 --> 00:02:34,682
with the next person named.

45
00:02:36,381 --> 00:02:37,256
Step two.

46
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:39,939
Amina opens the Approval inbox

47
00:02:39,989 --> 00:02:42,923
and searches Dubai client workshop.

48
00:02:43,128 --> 00:02:46,801
Omar's request appears with the same details he submitted.

49
00:02:47,020 --> 00:02:49,315
She reviews them and approves.

50
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:52,185
Omar cannot approve his own request,

51
00:02:52,418 --> 00:02:55,905
so QueueForge saves Amina's independent decision

52
00:02:55,955 --> 00:02:58,079
before processing begins.

53
00:02:59,381 --> 00:03:00,454
Step three.

54
00:03:00,659 --> 00:03:03,210
QueueForge starts the background work.

55
00:03:03,415 --> 00:03:07,131
The first attempt fails, but it remains visible.

56
00:03:07,349 --> 00:03:11,832
QueueForge tries again, and the second attempt succeeds.

57
00:03:12,037 --> 00:03:16,520
It creates a signed receipt so the completed result can be verified,

58
00:03:16,724 --> 00:03:17,841
then delivers it.

59
00:03:18,045 --> 00:03:22,470
In Delivery connections, the administrator searches Expense review

60
00:03:22,520 --> 00:03:24,716
and finds the matching send.

61
00:03:24,935 --> 00:03:28,253
The delivery record confirms it was accepted,

62
00:03:28,443 --> 00:03:32,088
and Omar's request now reads Completed.

63
00:03:33,381 --> 00:03:36,102
Omar receives a completion notification.

64
00:03:36,307 --> 00:03:40,690
In Activity log, the administrator searches Request completed

65
00:03:40,753 --> 00:03:43,957
and finds the matching 12:42 entry.

66
00:03:44,148 --> 00:03:47,707
Opening that row reveals who changed the request,

67
00:03:47,912 --> 00:03:50,618
when it happened, and the saved evidence needed

68
00:03:50,668 --> 00:03:53,645
to understand the retry and final outcome.

69
00:03:53,878 --> 00:03:57,864
One request now reads as one clear story.

70
00:04:01,395 --> 00:04:05,409
Behind the screen, QueueForge confirms Omar may act

71
00:04:05,543 --> 00:04:08,335
and saves the request in its database.

72
00:04:08,540 --> 00:04:11,361
A background worker performs the task,

73
00:04:11,594 --> 00:04:14,685
then passes the result to the receiving system.

74
00:04:14,889 --> 00:04:18,674
As each step finishes, the website updates the status

75
00:04:18,724 --> 00:04:20,239
and keeps the history.

76
00:04:22,409 --> 00:04:27,601
From the request, to the independent decision, retry, delivery,

77
00:04:27,651 --> 00:04:32,487
and final record, QueueForge keeps every handoff understandable

78
00:04:32,537 --> 00:04:34,165
and leaves proof behind.
